<h1>Forecasting hypoxia and algal blooms in estuaries</h1>
<p class="lead">My PhD research at North Carolina State University: statistical and mechanistic models that forecast, and help prevent nutrient-driven water quality problems in estuaries and coastal waters.</p>
<p>Eutrophication is the enrichment of natural waters with nutrients from human activity, such as agricultural fertilizer and wastewater. It promotes harmful algal blooms and hypoxia (dissolved oxygen below 2 mg/L), which damage fisheries, ecosystems and water supplies. <a href="https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/eutrophication.html">According to NOAA</a>, 65% of the estuaries and coastal waters in the US are moderately to severely degraded by excess nutrients, and the conservative estimate of <a href="https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/es801217q">damage from eutrophication in US freshwaters alone</a> is about $2.2 billion a year.</p>
<p class="toc"><a href="#neuse">Neuse River Estuary</a><a href="#gulf">Gulf of Mexico</a><a href="#pubs">Publications</a></p>

<h2 id="neuse">Neuse River Estuary</h2>
<p>The Neuse River Estuary in southeastern North Carolina has had hypoxia, algal blooms and fish kills for over two decades, attributed to rising nutrient loads from a fast-growing watershed. I built mechanistic and empirical models of near-bottom dissolved oxygen and of chlorophyll a, a proxy for algal blooms.</p>
<figure class="fig w-md"><a href="/img/research/neuse_map_osm.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="/img/research/neuse_map_osm.jpg" alt="Map of the Neuse River Estuary from New Bern to Pamlico Sound, North Carolina, with a North Carolina locator inset"></a>
<figcaption>The Neuse River Estuary, North Carolina.</figcaption></figure>

<h3>Hypoxia</h3>
<p>A process-based biophysical model describes, with differential equations, how hydrologic, meteorologic and human influences drive near-bottom oxygen in time and space. It is calibrated in a Bayesian framework, which estimates uncertain rate parameters from prior scientific knowledge and historical data.</p>
<p>Higher winter river discharge increases flushing and leaves less time to produce and settle organic matter, which raises oxygen in summer. Sediments are the dominant oxygen sink, but hypoxia can still be reduced by seasonal cuts in nutrient loading.</p>
<p>Because the predictions are probabilistic, they give the chance of seeing an improvement under different nutrient regulations. With a 30% loading reduction and sampling twice a month (current practice), it would take about 10 years to be 90% confident of an increase in mean May-October near-bottom oxygen.</p>
<figure class="fig w-sm"><a href="/img/research/neuse_years_to_improve.png"><img loading="lazy" src="/img/research/neuse_years_to_improve.png" alt="Probability of observing an oxygen increase over 1 to 10 years for 30, 50 and 70 percent nutrient loading reductions"></a>
<figcaption>Probability of observing an increase in mean May-October dissolved oxygen compared with average 1997-2015 conditions, for three nutrient loading reductions. Solid lines: the estuary sampled every day; dashed lines: sampled every two weeks.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The same model forecasts hypoxia from river flow and nutrient loads. I produced the annual summer Neuse hypoxia forecast starting in 2018:</p>
