<h3>Algal blooms</h3>
<p>I built and compared a hierarchical piecewise regression and a mechanistic model of chlorophyll a, both calibrated with Bayesian inference (Hamiltonian Monte Carlo in Stan for the regression, an adaptive Metropolis algorithm for the mechanistic model). The regression found a strong effect of river discharge, with a change point that depends on the size of the flow. For nutrient management scenarios the process-based model is preferable, because it describes the biochemical mechanisms explicitly.</p>
<p>Nitrogen is the main nutrient limiting phytoplankton growth, even under phosphorus reductions, and the limitation is stronger in dry conditions. Reducing nutrient concentrations, rather than total loads, is the key to controlling chlorophyll a. The process-based model predicts that a 10% change in nitrogen loading (flow held constant) changes estuary chlorophyll a by about 4.3%; the regression implied a larger and likely unrealistic 10%.</p>
<figure class="fig w-xs"><a href="/img/research/neuse_chla_scenarios.png"><img loading="lazy" src="/img/research/neuse_chla_scenarios.png" alt="Chlorophyll a predictions under nutrient loading changes from minus 30 to plus 30 percent, by season and flow condition, for the regression and process-based models"></a>
<figcaption>Average chlorophyll a predictions from the hierarchical regression and the process-based model, by season and hydrologic condition, under nutrient loading changes (river nutrient concentrations changed by the percent shown, flows held constant). 90% credible intervals shown for summer only.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Taken together, a 30% nutrient loading reduction would lower chlorophyll a by about 14% and the number of hypoxic days by about 25%.</p>

<h2 id="gulf">Gulf of Mexico</h2>
<p>A low-oxygen zone forms each summer in the northern Gulf of Mexico and affects the distribution of commercially important fish and shellfish. Most <a href="https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1705293114">existing forecasts</a> use only spring nitrogen load and report only late-July conditions, while fisheries are active across the whole summer.</p>

<h3>Daily hypoxia forecasts</h3>
<p>I built a forecasting system on a Bayesian mechanistic model that gives dissolved oxygen and hypoxic area at daily resolution, with up to four months of lead time, and separates the uncertainty coming from parameters, river and weather inputs, and model error. Constraining summer river inputs with spring conditions, including precipitation over the Mississippi River basin, improves forecast skill, and spring wind data improves it further. Retrospective forecasts explain about 50% of the temporal variability in hypoxic area for both sections of the Louisiana-Texas shelf (1985-2016).</p>
<figure class="fig"><a href="/img/research/gulf_west_1993.png"><img loading="lazy" src="/img/research/gulf_west_1993.png" alt="Daily pseudo-forecast of hypoxic area, west section, 1993"></a>
<a href="/img/research/gulf_east_1993.png"><img loading="lazy" src="/img/research/gulf_east_1993.png" alt="Daily pseudo-forecast of hypoxic area, east section, 1993" style="margin-top:12px"></a>
<figcaption>Daily pseudo-forecasts of hypoxic area for the west and east sections of the northern Gulf in 1993. Grey bands: 95% predictive range, split into parameter, input data, model error and adjustment uncertainty (light to dark). Yellow dashed line: hindcast; black dashed line: 32-year average hindcast; orange points: observations with 95% confidence intervals.</figcaption></figure>

<h3>Shrimp distributions</h3>
<p>Models of brown and white shrimp on the Louisiana shelf, linking their spatial distribution to hypoxia and other environmental, water quality and human factors, fitted to survey data with Bayesian statistical models. Published in 2025 (see below).</p>
